WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.338
You've seen three case studies in this module.

00:07.338 --> 00:09.800
In every one, there was a moment when someone could

00:09.800 --> 00:12.687
have stopped and said, this isn't right.

00:12.687 --> 00:15.187
This lesson is about that moment. When to stop

00:15.187 --> 00:18.613
a job, how to say it, who to tell, and what happens

00:18.613 --> 00:19.462
next.

00:20.593 --> 00:23.143
Stopping unsafe work is your right under federal

00:23.143 --> 00:26.380
law. At Allen, it's also your job. Every miner

00:26.380 --> 00:29.068
is expected to report hazards right away,

00:29.068 --> 00:31.367
and never assume someone else already did.

00:32.490 --> 00:35.378
Who do you tell? Start with your lead or the supervisor

00:35.378 --> 00:38.153
on your shift. At every site the line runs from

00:38.153 --> 00:40.753
the mine foreman, to the site supervisor,

00:40.753 --> 00:43.991
to Hunter Kenney, who is over all of our sites.

00:43.991 --> 00:46.840
Right now, none of our sites has a designated miners'

00:46.840 --> 00:49.478
representative, so your path is your lead,

00:49.478 --> 00:51.791
your foreman, your site supervisor,

00:51.791 --> 00:54.078
Hunter Kenney or the safety manager,

00:54.078 --> 00:57.303
and, if you ever need it, MSHA. Know those names

00:57.303 --> 00:58.490
before you need them.

00:59.616 --> 01:01.928
In Module 2 you learned your rights under the Mine

01:01.928 --> 01:06.791
Act. Section 105(c) protects you from being fired,

01:06.791 --> 01:09.291
punished, or discriminated against for reporting

01:09.291 --> 01:13.291
a hazard or a violation, to Allen or to MSHA,

01:13.291 --> 01:15.478
and for using your other safety rights.

01:15.478 --> 01:17.553
That protection has been applied to miners who

01:17.553 --> 01:20.491
refuse work they honestly and reasonably believe

01:20.491 --> 01:23.228
is unsafe, and who tell the company why.

01:24.354 --> 01:26.741
If you believe you've been retaliated against,

01:26.741 --> 01:29.379
you can file a complaint with MSHA within sixty

01:29.379 --> 01:32.629
days. You can also ask MSHA for an inspection if

01:32.629 --> 01:34.841
you believe there's a violation or an imminent

01:34.841 --> 01:37.629
danger, and MSHA will keep your name confidential

01:37.629 --> 01:38.691
if you ask.

01:42.004 --> 01:44.566
Stop when you haven't been trained for the task.

01:44.566 --> 01:47.304
Part 48 says you get task training before you do

01:47.304 --> 01:50.054
a new task, so if you're asked to run a machine

01:50.054 --> 01:52.316
or do a job you haven't been trained on,

01:52.316 --> 01:55.591
say so. Stop when a hazard isn't controlled,

01:55.591 --> 01:58.041
like loose ground, a missing guard,

01:58.041 --> 02:01.191
a machine that isn't locked out, or a low berm.

02:01.191 --> 02:03.929
Stop when something changes, like the ground,

02:03.929 --> 02:06.579
the weather, or the equipment. And stop when you're

02:06.579 --> 02:09.104
simply not sure. Not sure is a good enough reason

02:09.104 --> 02:10.042
to ask.

02:11.158 --> 02:13.333
If it's an imminent danger, something that could

02:13.333 --> 02:15.870
kill or seriously hurt someone before it can be

02:15.870 --> 02:19.133
fixed, don't debate it. Get yourself and others

02:19.133 --> 02:21.708
out of the area first, then report it.

02:21.708 --> 02:24.370
The workplace exam rule requires the area to be

02:24.370 --> 02:27.445
cleared until the danger is fixed, and nobody goes

02:27.445 --> 02:29.720
back except the people fixing it.

02:30.845 --> 02:32.769
Stopping is hardest when you're new.

02:32.769 --> 02:35.544
You don't want to look slow. The crew is behind.

02:35.544 --> 02:37.857
Someone says it's always been done this way.

02:37.857 --> 02:40.707
Those are exactly the moments in the case studies.

02:40.707 --> 02:43.494
Experienced miners did a familiar job the way they'd

02:43.494 --> 02:44.669
done it before.

02:45.815 --> 02:48.040
Being new is actually an advantage here.

02:48.040 --> 02:50.589
You see things with fresh eyes, and you're expected

02:50.589 --> 02:53.665
to ask questions. Nobody expects a new miner to

02:53.665 --> 02:56.464
know everything. Everyone expects a new miner to

02:56.464 --> 02:57.440
speak up.

03:00.789 --> 03:03.489
Here's how to stop a job. First, stop in a safe

03:03.489 --> 03:05.951
way. Put the machine in a safe state,

03:05.951 --> 03:08.626
set the brake, and step out of the hazard.

03:08.626 --> 03:11.689
Second, warn anyone else who could be exposed.

03:11.689 --> 03:14.839
Third, tell your lead or supervisor right away,

03:14.839 --> 03:18.276
in person or by radio. Fourth, say exactly what

03:18.276 --> 03:20.689
you saw and why you think it's unsafe.

03:20.689 --> 03:23.339
And fifth, agree on what happens next,

03:23.339 --> 03:25.726
whether that's a fix, a different method,

03:25.726 --> 03:27.539
or someone trained to take over.

03:28.683 --> 03:32.020
Be specific. "The tail guard on this belt is missing"

03:32.020 --> 03:34.957
gets action faster than "this seems sketchy."

03:34.957 --> 03:37.607
Keep it about the hazard, not about a person.

03:38.733 --> 03:41.320
Here's an example. A new miner is told to clear

03:41.320 --> 03:44.345
spill under a belt. He walks over and sees the

03:44.345 --> 03:46.795
belt is stopped but not locked out.

03:46.795 --> 03:49.608
He tells the lead: the belt isn't locked out,

03:49.608 --> 03:52.132
and I'm not going under it until my lock is on.

03:52.132 --> 03:54.583
The lead says, it'll only take a minute.

03:54.583 --> 03:56.933
The new miner says, I'll do it as soon as it's

03:56.933 --> 03:59.583
locked out. Can you show me the disconnect?

04:00.698 --> 04:02.823
The lead walks him to the disconnect.

04:02.823 --> 04:05.123
They lock it out together, he puts on his own lock

04:05.123 --> 04:08.436
and tag, they try the start, and he does the job.

04:08.436 --> 04:10.898
Nobody was in trouble. The job got done the right

04:10.898 --> 04:13.711
way, and it took about three extra minutes.

04:17.028 --> 04:19.728
After you stop a job, the hazard gets fixed or

04:19.728 --> 04:22.278
controlled before work goes on, and it goes on

04:22.278 --> 04:25.453
the record. At Allen, you can always report a hazard

04:25.453 --> 04:27.791
by telling your supervisor, and you can log it

04:27.791 --> 04:30.741
as an observation in the HCSS app, which goes to

04:30.741 --> 04:33.753
upper management. Equipment defects go on the pre-trip

04:33.753 --> 04:36.691
inspection list or app. If you're not satisfied

04:36.691 --> 04:38.728
with the answer, you can take it higher,

04:38.728 --> 04:41.603
to the site supervisor, Hunter Kenney or the safety

04:41.603 --> 04:44.266
manager. And you always have the right to contact

04:44.266 --> 04:45.341
MSHA.

04:46.470 --> 04:48.458
What if you're told to keep going anyway?

04:48.458 --> 04:51.483
Stay calm and stay specific. Repeat what the hazard

04:51.483 --> 04:53.995
is and what would make it safe. Ask who else can

04:53.995 --> 04:56.870
look at it. Don't do the unsafe part of the job

04:56.870 --> 04:59.095
while it's being sorted out. And write down what

04:59.095 --> 05:01.658
happened and when, while it's fresh.

05:02.797 --> 05:05.372
To sum up. Stop when you're not trained,

05:05.372 --> 05:07.747
when a hazard isn't controlled, when something

05:07.747 --> 05:10.122
has changed, or when you're not sure.

05:10.122 --> 05:13.209
Stop safely, warn others, tell your lead,

05:13.209 --> 05:15.997
and say exactly what and why. The law protects

05:15.997 --> 05:18.809
you for raising safety concerns. And a good crew

05:18.809 --> 05:20.109
will thank you for it.

05:21.234 --> 05:24.397
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

05:24.397 --> 05:25.897
override this video.
